      "content": "I'll try to keep the goblin references to a minimum. To continue, if the pitot tube becomes clogged with debris, the airspeed reading will be reduced or even become stuck. This is because the clog restricts airflow, reducing the pressure difference between the stagnation and static pressures.\n\nIn this scenario, the airspeed indicator will display a lower-than-actual airspeed reading, which can lead to a range of issues, including reduced control authority and potentially even stall warnings. It's a serious situation, as pilots rely on accurate airspeed readings to maintain control and navigate safely.\n\nIn fact, a clogged pitot tube can be particularly problematic in icing conditions, as the ice accumulation can further reduce the pressure difference and exacerbate the issue. It's a situation that requires prompt attention and correction to ensure safe flight operations.",
